      "narrative": "The rain hisses against the shuttered windows of the Copper Finch. Mara Venn leans back in her chair, the copper lamplight catching the sharp line of her jaw as she regards you over the rim of her cup.\n\n\"You want to know about the Ash Gate.\" She sets the cup down, the clay thudding against scarred wood. Her eyes narrow \u2014 not with suspicion, but with the particular focus of someone weighing how much to say.\n\nShe opens her mouth\u2014\n\nAnd Toran Vex, the nervous clerk at the next table, knocks over his inkwell. Black liquid spreads across a sheaf of papers. \"Damnation\u2014\" he hisses, fumbling with a rag.\n\nMara's attention flicks to him for half a breath. When her eyes return to you, something has shifted. Her posture is looser, almost dismissive.\n\n\"The Ash Gate,\" she says slowly, \"has been sealed longer than most folk in Thornfield have been alive. That's all most people know. That's all *I* know, really.\" She shrugs, the gesture too casual. \"Old Bram worked the ferry, back when there was passage. He's dead now. So the story dies with him.\"\n\nShe's lying. You can *feel* it \u2014 the way her words land too flat, the way her eyes don't hold yours. But you can't pin down what she's hiding. There's a shape to the omission, a gap in her story, but it slips through your fingers like smoke.\n\nThe silence stretches. Rain drums against the glass. Toran mops at his papers, muttering.\n\nMara picks up her cup again. \"Is there something else you wanted, traveler?\""
    },
