      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the warmth of lamplight and the low mutter of its patrons. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp, cool, carrying the smell of wet stone and woodsmoke from chimneys further up the street.\n\nThornfield is quiet. A few figures move between buildings, heads down against the drizzle, but no one pays you any mind. The road curves past the last few houses and opens onto a stretch of grey-green field, untended, overgrown with thistle and bramble.\n\nAnd there it stands.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises against the pale sky \u2014 a cracked black arch of stone, perhaps twelve feet tall, set into nothing. No wall, no fence, no structure supports it. It simply *stands* on the uneven ground like a door someone forgot to build a room around. The stone is dark with moisture, veined with lighter cracks that spiderweb across its surface. The archway itself holds nothing but the grey light of the morning \u2014 no shimmer, no barrier, no visible sign of what sealed it.\n\nBut where the mechanism would be \u2014 where a lock or latch might sit on any ordinary gate \u2014 there's a recess in the stone. A cavity, roughly the size of a fist, worn smooth at the edges. Empty.\n\nAt the base of the gate, half-hidden in the wet grass, a small bundle of cloth lies crumpled. A travel-stained cloak, discarded."
    },
