      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch, fingers brushing the wet blades as you sweep your gaze across the ground beneath the arch. The grass is flattened in places \u2014 from weather, from your own boots, from the passage of whoever left the cloak. You part a clump of stems, peer into the shadow where the stone meets the earth, trace the line where rainwater has pooled in a shallow dip.\n\nNothing.\n\nJust wet grass, scattered leaves carried by the wind, the dark glisten of mud. A few pebbles. A worm, disturbed, slowly curling back into the soil. The dirt beneath the arch is packed firm, undisturbed by anything recent \u2014 no fresh dig marks, no scrapes, no hidden cache revealed.\n\nYou rise, your knees damp, your palms streaked with moisture and a faint green stain from crushed grass. The gate looms overhead, silent and indifferent. Whatever it hides \u2014 if it hides anything \u2014 isn't written in the ground at your feet.\n\nThe crumpled cloak still lies a few paces away, a darker shape against the pale grass. The only thing out of place in this quiet field."
