      "narrative": "The walk back is a short one \u2014 past the last crooked fence posts, past the rain barrel outside the smithy, past a young woman hauling a basket of wet laundry across the street. The drizzle has softened to a mist, clinging to your hair and cloak.\n\nThe Copper Finch's sign creaks overhead as you push the door open. The warmth hits you again \u2014 the hearth, the bodies, the smell of old ale and woodsmoke. A few heads turn, note your return, and look away. The morning crowd has thinned slightly; a pair of farmers nurse cups by the window, and a lone figure in a patched coat reads a crumpled broadsheet near the fire.\n\nYour table \u2014 the scarred one near the back wall, within arm's reach of the hearth's warmth \u2014 sits empty. A half-melted candle stub in a tin holder. A ring from a wet glass, already drying.\n\nAs you settle into the chair, the wood creaking under you, you notice Mara Venn behind the bar. She's wiping a cup with a rag, her movements unhurried. She glances up, sees you, and holds your gaze for one beat \u2014 two \u2014 before returning to her work without a word.\n\nToran Vex is nowhere in sight. The back door to the kitchen swings gently, as if someone passed through it moments ago."
